<!--
  jt-ioc: threat-listed addresses in generic fields and JSON web access logs
  File:    01-906000-jason_tools_ioc.xml
  Range:   906001-906099, shared with zz-906050-jason_tools_ioc_late.xml
           (this file: 906001-906028, 906040-906046, 906061-906079)
  Licence: AGPL-3.0-or-later    Jason Tools Co., Ltd.

  Two lists, both written by etc/jt-packs/bin/update-threat-ip-list.py:
    jason_tools_threat_ip       every listed address: the "one source" tier
    jason_tools_threat_ip_high  two independent providers or a curated source
                                (Spamhaus DROP; ThreatFox / Feodo when enabled)
  Each lookup of the first list has a child that looks the same field up in the
  second. A value check on one list would have been simpler, but
  address_match_key_value is inverted in 4.x (lists_list.c; wazuh/wazuh#8269,
  re-measured on 4.14.7 on 2026-10-11), so the tier needs its own list. On a
  node where the second list is not declared, analysisd ignores only the
  children (warning 7616) and the first tier keeps working.

  Why the first tier is quiet: on 2026-10-11 91 per cent of the 1.4 list came
  from a single provider, most of it scanners and months-old bans. A listed
  source on its own is the background of the internet, not an incident.

  The rules in this file hang off built-in rule 1, so they see only events that
  no specific rule chain takes (JSON logs without their own rules, generic
  syslog). sshd, Windows, Sysmon, Suricata and web access logs in combined
  format are handled in the zz- file.

  History: 1.3 moved logins and Sysmon to the zz- file and graded web requests;
  1.5 adds the tiers, drops 906080 (a child without a condition, which made
  every destination match level 14) and the tactic id TA0011.
-->

